Make array use of a for-in variable an error rather than a crash.
The variable's walker list shares a union with the parent pointer of a
function argument and with the array pointer. So after "for (p in A)"
on an argument p, an array use of p such as "p[1] = 1" followed the
walker list, or NULL after the loop, as the parent variable and
crashed. On a global k, "k[1]" in the loop body replaced the walker
list with an array, and "for (k in A)" with k already an array treated
the walker list as k's elements; both crashed too. Now iamarray() stops
at a walked argument, as length() does, and both uses are errors, as in
other awks, since a for-in variable is a scalar.

---
 editors/awk.c |   10 +++++++++-
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/editors/awk.c
+++ b/editors/awk.c
@@ -1042,10 +1042,14 @@
 
 static xhash *iamarray(var *a)
 {
-	while (a->type & VF_CHILD)
+	/* A "for (arg in A)" walker reuses x.parent */
+	while ((a->type & (VF_CHILD | VF_WALK)) == VF_CHILD)
 		a = a->x.parent;
 
 	if (!(a->type & VF_ARRAY)) {
+		/* ...and x.array: a for-in variable is a scalar */
+		if (a->type & VF_WALK)
+			syntax_error(EMSG_NOT_ARRAY);
 		a->type |= VF_ARRAY;
 		a->x.array = hash_init();
 	}
@@ -2342,6 +2346,10 @@
 	walker_list *w;
 	walker_list *prev_walker;
 
+	/* The walker would overwrite x.array */
+	if (v->type & VF_ARRAY)
+		syntax_error("Array used as scalar");
+
 	if (v->type & VF_WALK) {
 		prev_walker = v->x.walker;
 	} else {
